@*
    The extension namespace: every `ext.<plugin>.<key>` a block carries, drawn as
    one row rather than one row per key.

    One row because of what the keys are. They belong to plugins layered on top of
    devbook, not to devbook, and the convention carries them untouched: no rule
    about them, no meaning this library could draw, no edge. Given a row each they
    would sit among the fields as though they were schema — which the rule forbids
    anyone reading them as — and a plugin that remembers four things about a
    chapter would out-shout its status. Gathered under one `ext` label they read as
    what they are: somebody else's notes, visibly present and visibly not ours.

    Shown rather than collapsed behind a disclosure. There are rarely more than a
    few, a disclosure is a control in the middle of a description list, and a
    hidden value is exactly the invisible-field problem MetadataExtraFields exists
    to avoid.

    Each entry is `key: value` exactly as written, the key being what follows
    `ext.` with the author's casing. An empty value is kept and drawn as the key
    and its colon — the omit-when-empty rule does not reach this namespace, so
    `ext.some-plugin.seen:` is a statement too. The tooltip carries the full key a
    reader would search the fence for.
*@

@foreach (var (key, value) in Ext)
{
    <MetadataScalar Value="@Entry(key, value)" Title="@($"{DevbookSchema.ExtensionPrefix}{key}")" ValueCssClass="@ValueCssClass" />
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The extension keys (without <c>ext.</c>) and their verbatim
    /// values. Empty draws nothing; whether the row belongs is the caller's
    /// question.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> Ext { get; set; } = new Dictionary<string, string>();

    [Parameter]
    public string? ValueCssClass { get; set; } = "devbook-value";

    private static string Entry(string key, string value) =>
        value.Length == 0 ? $"{key}:" : $"{key}: {value}";
}
